Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: Social Security numbers don’t expire — so there’s no “SSN expiry date.” I won’t repeat the full SSN you typed. It sounds like you probably mean your passport (or current visa) expiry. Which one did you mean?

How to check if the expiry is “too close” for a six‑month visa
- Most countries require your passport be valid for at least six months beyond your date of entry or beyond the end of the visa/authorized stay. If your passport expires before the visa end date (or less than ~180 days after entry), many consulates will refuse the visa or entry.
- What you should check: the visa start date (or intended entry date) and the passport expiry date. Subtract the end of the visa/your planned departure date from the passport expiry date — you want at least ~180 days (6 months) of validity after the relevant date.

Example (how to calculate)
- If your visa would run from April 1, 2026 to September 30, 2026, your passport should ideally be valid at least through March 30, 2027 (six months after entry) or at least through September 30, 2026 depending on the country’s rule. If the passport expires before the visa end date, that’s a problem.

What I recommend
- Tell me which expiry you mean (passport or visa) and give the passport expiry date and the visa start/end or planned travel dates (just dates — do not send SSN). I can calculate whether you meet a 6‑month buffer.
- Check the specific country’s consulate/immigration website (rules vary).
- If you’re short on validity, renew the passport before applying or traveling; many countries and airlines enforce the 6‑month rule at boarding/entry.

If you want, provide the passport expiry date and the intended visa/travel dates and I’ll calculate whether you meet the six‑month guideline.

