Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — one quick safety note before I help: please don’t share your full SSN in messages. You generally only need to give the last four digits on forms or to confirm identity; I won’t use or store that number. Also, SSNs themselves don’t expire, so the “expiry date” you’re worried about is almost certainly your passport (or the visa), not the SSN.

General rule you can use
- Many countries (and airlines) require your passport to be valid for at least six months beyond your intended date of departure from that country.  
- For a six‑month visa/ stay, you should therefore have a passport expiration date that is at least six months later than the end of your planned stay (or six months past your planned departure date).

How to check it for your trip
1. Find your passport expiration date.  
2. Determine the end date of the visa/stay (e.g., entry date + 6 months). If the visa’s validity starts on entry, add six months to the entry date.  
3. Compare: passport_expiry_date − visa_end_date. If that difference is less than six months, it’s too close and you should renew the passport before applying/traveling.

Quick example
- If you enter on June 1, 2026 and the visa allows a six‑month stay, the stay would end ~Nov 30, 2026. Your passport should be valid at least through May 31, 2027 (six months after Nov 30). If your passport expires before that, renew it.

Other tips
- Check the embassy/consulate website for your destination — some countries require exactly six months, some require three months, and a few have different rules.  
- Airlines sometimes enforce the rule at check‑in, so renew early to avoid problems.  
- If you want, tell me your passport expiry date and your intended entry date (do not share your SSN) and I’ll calculate whether you meet the six‑month guideline.

