Quick safety note: please avoid posting your full SSN (or other sensitive numbers) in chats — I won’t store it, but it’s best not to share. For this question I don’t need your SSN; I only need the passport expiry date and destination (or travel dates).

Do you mean the passport expiry date? If so, here’s the short answer and what to do next:

- Short answer: Many countries require your passport to be valid for at least 6 months beyond your planned entry or departure date for a visa or to board a flight. If your passport expires within six months of when you plan to travel or while the visa would still be in effect, that’s often considered “too close” and you should renew before applying or traveling.  
- How to check: take your travel start date (or visa start date) and add 6 months. If your passport expiry is on or before that date, renew it first. Example: if you enter on July 1, 2026, your passport should be valid at least through January 1, 2027 (preferably later).
- Exceptions/variations: Some countries require only 3 months, some have other rules—always check the embassy/consulate website for the destination country or the visa guidance page.
- If it’s too close: renew your passport ASAP (many services offer expedited renewal). Also contact the consulate or visa office if you already applied — they can advise if your application will be affected.

If you’d like, tell me:
- the destination country (and whether you mean the visa validity or your planned travel dates), and
- your passport expiry date (don’t send SSN or other sensitive numbers)

I can then calculate whether the expiry meets a 6-month requirement and point you to the embassy/consulate guidance or help draft a renewal/email if needed.

