Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
First — do not share your SSN in chats. I won’t repeat it, and you don’t need to include it here.

Quick clarification: Social Security Numbers (SSNs) don’t expire, so the “expiry date” you mention is almost certainly your passport’s expiry. Can you confirm you mean your passport? If so, tell me:
- Which country you’re applying to (or the embassy/consulate), and
- Your planned travel / visa start date and the passport expiry date.

General guidance (useful immediately):
- Most countries require your passport to be valid for at least six months beyond your intended date of departure from that country. Some require more (e.g., 3–12 months extra) or require validity through the full visa validity.
- If you’re applying for a visa that will be valid for six months, many embassies expect the passport to be valid for the entire visa period plus an extra buffer. That often means passport expiry > visa end date + 3–6 months.
- Practical rule of thumb: have at least 6 months of validity beyond your planned stay; 12 months is safer if you can renew.
- Also consider processing time: if your passport will expire during visa processing, you may be asked to renew first.

Examples:
- If your trip/visa runs June 1 → Dec 1 (six months), passport should generally be valid through at least Dec 1 + 3–6 months (so into March–June of the next year) for many countries.
- If your passport expires sooner than the six-month buffer, you should renew before applying.

What I can do next:
- Check the specific embassy/consulate requirement if you tell me the destination country.
- Help you decide whether to renew now (based on your passport expiry + travel dates).
- Draft an email to the consulate or airline if you want to confirm their rule.

Tell me the country and the passport expiry date (and planned travel/visa dates) and I’ll give a specific recommendation.

