- If you meant your passport expiry (most likely when applying for a visa), read the rest of this answer.

2) Passport / visa validity rule (common guidance)
- Many countries require your passport to be valid for at least 6 months beyond the end of your intended stay or beyond the validity of your visa. In other words:
  Passport expiry date should be >= (your planned departure date + 6 months).
- Example: if you plan to enter on June 1 and stay the full six months (leaving ~Nov 30), your passport should usually be valid through at least the end of May the following year (about 6 months after Nov 30).

3) What to do if your expiry is “too close”
- If your passport would expire within that 6‑month buffer, renew it before applying or traveling. Airlines and border officials can deny boarding or entry for insufficient passport validity.
- If the “expiry” is an SSN card printed date: check your immigration/work authorization status. If your work authorization (EAD, visa status) is expiring, you’ll need to renew that through USCIS/your employer’s process — the SSN number itself isn’t reissued.

4) Next steps I can help with
- Tell me which document’s expiry you’re referring to (passport or SSN card) and the expiry date and which country the visa is for. I can:
  - Check the consulate/embassy requirement for that country
  - Tell you whether the date looks acceptable for a six‑month visa
  - Suggest renewal timing and steps

If you want, share the passport expiry date and the country of the visa (don’t share the full SSN), and I’ll give a direct yes/no and recommended actions.

