I won’t repeat your full SSN here, but quick important notes first: SSNs do not expire, and you should avoid sharing the full number on forms or messages unless the site is secure and the request is legitimate — using only the last four digits is safer when possible.

A couple quick clarifying questions:
- Which “expiry date” do you mean — your passport, a travel document, or something else?
- Which country’s visa are you applying for and what are your intended travel dates (or the visa length)?

General guidance you can use right away
- If you mean your passport: many countries require a passport to be valid for at least six months beyond your date of entry or beyond your planned departure date. If your passport expires less than six months after your planned stay (or the visa end date), that is often considered “too close” and can cause a visa denial or airline refusal.
- If you mean some other document: SSNs don’t expire; driver’s licenses and IDs have their own rules, but for international travel the passport is the critical one.
- For a six-month visa specifically: you generally need your passport to remain valid for the visa’s full period plus the country’s six‑month buffer (requirements vary). In short — if the passport would expire during the visa period or within six months after it ends, renew the passport first.

What I recommend
- If you share (only) your passport expiry date and the destination/country, I can check typical embassy/consulate requirements and tell you whether it looks safe or if you should renew first.
- If your passport is within 6–12 months of expiry, consider renewing before applying. Processing times vary; expedited services are available in many countries.
- Don’t put your full SSN into forms that aren’t explicitly secure; use last-4 if acceptable, and verify the site’s legitimacy.

If you want, tell me the passport expiry date and the country (or paste the visa instructions you see) and I’ll give a concrete yes/no and next steps.

