Quick clarification before I advise: when you say “expiry date,” do you mean your passport’s expiry or the SSN card/record? (I won’t repeat the SSN you typed — I’ll only reference the last four if needed.)

Key points to know
- Social Security numbers themselves do not expire. If you meant the SSN, there’s no “expiry date” for the number. (If you have a Social Security card issued to a non‑citizen, the card can show work‑authorization limits — that’s different from the SSN itself.)
- Passport expiry is what matters for most visas and entry rules. Many countries require your passport to be valid for at least the length of your stay plus an additional margin (commonly 3–6 months beyond your planned departure). If you’re applying for a six‑month visa, some consulates expect the passport to be valid at least six months after the visa start or after your planned departure.

What to do next
1. Tell me which country’s visa you’re applying for and the passport expiry date (just month/year is fine). I can check the typical rule and tell you whether that expiry looks too close.
2. If your passport doesn’t meet the requirement: renew it before applying. Check the issuing authority’s official site for renewal timing and expedited options — don’t rely on informal timelines.
3. If you actually meant a Social Security card that’s tied to DHS work authorization, tell me whether you’re a U.S. citizen or non‑citizen and the card’s stated restriction (if any), and I’ll explain implications.

Privacy note
- Avoid pasting full SSNs into messages. If you need help with forms that show the SSN field, give only the last four digits.

If you tell me which country and your passport expiry (month/year), I’ll give a direct answer about whether it’s too close for a six‑month visa and what to do.

